00:13:05.450 --> 00:13:07.680
But what about the liability
of people who break

00:13:07.680 --> 00:13:10.070
those quarantine and isolation orders?

00:13:10.070 --> 00:13:12.270
Well, we've now witnessed
state authorities using

00:13:12.270 --> 00:13:14.350
their broad powers to
issue orders requiring

00:13:14.350 --> 00:13:17.120
businesses to close,
people to remain at home,

00:13:17.120 --> 00:13:20.020
and sick patients to be
isolated and quarantined.

00:13:20.020 --> 00:13:23.040
But the unique nature of
this crisis has governors,

00:13:23.040 --> 00:13:25.380
mayors, and police making ad hoc decisions

00:13:25.380 --> 00:13:28.270
about the quarantine, and apart
from the criminal liability

00:13:28.270 --> 00:13:31.040
and potential jail time
of defying these orders,

00:13:31.040 --> 00:13:33.270
you might also be on the hook for damages

00:13:33.270 --> 00:13:36.530
caused by just people getting
sick from your actions,

00:13:36.530 --> 00:13:38.030
and I think there are
a couple avenues here.

00:13:38.030 --> 00:13:40.510
One is what's called negligence per se.

00:13:40.510 --> 00:13:43.210
Negligence per se is the
law's way of imposing

00:13:43.210 --> 00:13:46.270
strict liability on
specific circumstances.

00:13:46.270 --> 00:13:48.600
Strict liability is automatic liability.

00:13:48.600 --> 00:13:51.070
So this means that the
defendant is liable,

00:13:51.070 --> 00:13:52.880
regardless of the defendant's intent

00:13:52.880 --> 00:13:55.140
or what they knew or what
they should have known.

00:13:55.140 --> 00:13:57.660
If strict liability applies,
the defendant has to pay

00:13:57.660 --> 00:14:01.120
for his or her conduct if it
caused the plaintiff's injuries

00:14:01.120 --> 00:14:04.380
and one of the ways to
trigger negligence per se is

00:14:04.380 --> 00:14:05.770
when you break the law.

00:14:05.770 --> 00:14:08.250
If you break the law or
there is a statute that says

00:14:08.250 --> 00:14:10.630
you cannot conduct
these particular actions

00:14:10.630 --> 00:14:13.210
and you go and do that,
the law sometimes says that

00:14:13.210 --> 00:14:15.220
you have to pay for the
damage that you cause,

00:14:15.220 --> 00:14:17.980
regardless of whether you
acted negligently or not.

00:14:17.980 --> 00:14:20.370
So for example, in Connecticut
a woman was injured

00:14:20.370 --> 00:14:23.630
after a dog that was subject
to a 14 day quarantine escaped.

00:14:23.630 --> 00:14:25.700
The woman sued the local dog warden,

00:14:25.700 --> 00:14:27.277
and on appeal the court
of appeals held that

00:14:27.277 --> 00:14:30.900
the dog warden could be sued
and immunity did not apply

00:14:30.900 --> 00:14:33.720
because the dog catcher
violated the quarantine law,

00:14:33.720 --> 00:14:35.790
and in a somewhat analogous situation

00:14:35.790 --> 00:14:38.520
a Minnesota couple sued a
physician they hired to take care

00:14:38.520 --> 00:14:40.510
of their daughter who had scarlet fever.

00:14:40.510 --> 00:14:42.900
The physician allowed
the parents to visit her

00:14:42.900 --> 00:14:44.910
and take her home when her disease

00:14:44.910 --> 00:14:46.950
was at its most contagious,
advising them that

00:14:46.950 --> 00:14:48.670
she wasn't really contagious.

00:14:48.670 --> 00:14:50.860
The Minnesota Supreme
Court found that the doctor

00:14:50.860 --> 00:14:52.810
could be sued for negligence.

00:14:52.810 --> 00:14:54.370
So this is a fluid situation.

00:14:54.370 --> 00:14:56.580
You can probably expect
